**I would ship PROD-only, and backport it to the 32-token kernel**, provided the barrier wrappers have the release/acquire semantics you describe. This is a defensible correctness fix. The measurements strongly support it, although they do not yet identify the exact load that produced the garbage.

There is also a particularly useful CUTLASS precedent that resolves much of your concern.

For **question 1**, `PipelineTmaAsync::consumer_release()` itself omits the fence, but its callers can supply it. The SM90 epilogue does this after shared-to-register consumption, immediately before releasing the load buffer:

```cpp
cutlass::arch::fence_view_async_shared();
load_pipeline.consumer_release(load_pipe_consumer_state);
```

That is the `!ReuseSmemC` path. The helper emits exactly `fence.proxy.async.shared::cta` with a `"memory"` clobber. [CUTLASS epilogue](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/main/include/cutlass/epilogue/collective/sm90_epilogue_tma_warpspecialized.hpp#L735), [fence implementation](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/main/include/cutlass/arch/barrier.h#L723).

The usual TMA→WGMMA mainloop is a different comparison: the operand accesses use the async proxy, and the caller waits for the relevant WGMMA operations before releasing their buffers. Your `ldmatrix`/`ld.shared` consumption introduces generic-proxy reads. [CUTLASS mainloop](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/main/include/cutlass/gemm/collective/sm90_mma_tma_gmma_ss_warpspecialized.hpp#L496).

PTX’s cross-proxy requirement concerns memory accesses, including this read→write conflict. The implicit proxy fence associated with completed bulk copies covers observing their results; it occurs before your consumption and does not supply the later generic-read→async-overwrite edge. [PTX async-proxy semantics](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#asynchronous-copying-async-proxy).

Your two placements bridge the same synchronization path:

```text
CONS:
generic reads → proxy fence → warp sync → empty release
              → producer acquire → warp sync → async overwrite

PROD:
generic reads → warp sync → empty release
              → producer acquire → warp sync → proxy fence → async overwrite
```

A proxy fence can compose with the release/acquire chain at either end here because the participants are in the same CTA. NVIDIA’s proxy-model paper explicitly describes placement before release or after acquire, including either-thread placement within a CTA. Thus, **either half alone working is consistent with the intended semantics**, not inherently evidence of timing camouflage. [Mixed-Proxy Extensions, §5.3 and Figure 8](https://d1qx31qr3h6wln.cloudfront.net/publications/ISCA_2022_MixedProxy.pdf).

I prefer PROD because it centralizes the obligation before every async refill and executes in one warp instead of eight. Keep it in **all issuing producer lanes after the existing `__syncwarp()`**, as proposed. The three CONS sites are alternatives; they do not mean three fences per stage. Also, instruction cost is warp-based, so measure rather than interpreting this as 256 individually serialized fences.

That recommendation assumes:

- Each consumer warp releases exactly once per stage generation, after all its stage reads.
- `empty[s]` accounts for all eight consumer arrivals.
- Wait succeeds for the correct generation and has acquire semantics; arrival has release semantics.
- The inline-assembly wrappers correctly describe their compiler memory effects.

The fence cannot repair an incorrect arrival count, wrong phase, or a load genuinely executed after release.

For **question 2**, the start checks establish that the checked bytes matched at those checking accesses. They do not establish that later operand/metadata loads read those same bytes, nor that the global reference itself was correct. The post-check result is consistent with draining or delaying the vulnerable accesses, but is not localization.

These are the alternatives I would actively distinguish:

| Mechanism | Distinguishing experiment |
|---|---|
| **Compiler movement or incomplete inline-asm memory effects**, especially late `flush_meta` loads | Replace the fence with only `asm volatile("" ::: "memory")` at the same sites. Compare generated PTX and SASS, particularly the x2-scale loads versus empty arrival. A successful empty-clobber variant warrants investigating code generation; it does not alone prove the hardware fence unnecessary. |
| **Wrong empty-barrier count/phase or stage generation bookkeeping** | Track monotonically increasing generation numbers per physical stage and per consumer warp. Check the generation acknowledged by every arrival, rather than relying only on parity. Deliberately skew individual consumer warps. |
| **Steal/queue readiness publication or duplicate work ownership** | Disable `pre_ready` and perform the explicit acquire in every copying lane. Separately replay a recorded stage schedule with fixed, already-ready inputs, removing scheduler/activation publication from the experiment. |
| **Workspace lifetime or finalization overlap** | Run with ordinary stream dependencies, PDL disabled, and a separate workspace per invocation. A shared-memory fence changes timing enough to suppress this class of race without ordering it. |

My strongest experiment would be a **small stage-reuse reproducer with fixed sources**, the same eight consumer warps, and alternating shared/routed layouts. Retain delayed use of loaded metadata registers after empty release. Compare no fence, CONS, PROD, and compiler-clobber-only variants. Reproducing the failure there removes most scheduler and global-workspace explanations.

For `TD_NO_STEAL`, inspect the actual transition distribution. If it removes or reduces shared↔routed reuse of a physical stage, it directly suppresses the proposed trigger. Otherwise it can still alter producer lead, consumer skew, and readiness timing. **The relevant pair is the old and new occupant of the same stage—`STAGES` producer iterations apart—not adjacent scheduler entries.**

For **question 3**, there is a small correction and a stronger threshold observation.

Shared activation rows already overlap the routed **scale slot at T≤32**. T=33 starts overlapping routed XROW0. Offsets below are relative to `W_BYTES`:

| Routed field | Offset | First T whose shared copies touch it |
|---|---:|---:|
| Scale slot | 0–4095 | 1 |
| XROW0 activation data | 4096 | 33 |
| **XROW0 fp32 scale** | **5120** | **41** |
| XROW1 activation data | 5184 | 41 |
| XROW1 fp32 scale | 6208 | 49 |
| XROW2 fp32 scale | 7296 | 58 |

In particular:

```text
T=40: shared copies end at W_BYTES + 5120, exclusively.
T=41: shared copies include the row-0 x2 scale and start of XROW1.
```

**That makes the late x2-scale loads in `flush_meta` prime suspects.** Their results are needed for the subsequent flush, whereas most operand loads feed the preceding MMAs. They consequently have a different dependency/timing profile around release.

But I would not conclude that a corrupted x2 scale alone explains the enormous magnitude. The direction of contamination matters. For example, the finite FP16 bit pattern `0x7bff`, representing 65504, represents approximately **2.65×10³⁶ when interpreted as BF16**. Shared BF16 consumption encountering routed FP16 activation bytes therefore has a very plausible path to your observed magnitude. This is a bit-pattern explanation, not proof of the offending access.

There is a competing threshold effect: if `nt_n = ceil(T/8)`, T=41 also enables the **sixth shared token tile**. That changes executed loads/MMAs and timing.

A useful discriminating experiment is to shift the shared activation base by **+128 bytes**, updating both producer destinations and consumer addresses while retaining the same stage allocation. There is room in the layout shown. An overlap-driven boundary at T=41 should move to **T=40**; the sixth-token-tile boundary remains at 41. Also try isolating the late scale loads with a real register dependency before release, or moving their staged storage outside the shared activation footprint.

The overlap is legitimate storage reuse **when lifetimes are correctly ordered**. These thresholds identify newly vulnerable bytes; they do not make overlapping layouts intrinsically invalid.

For the shipped 32-token kernel: **yes, I would apply the same ordering fix**. Its protocol still has generic reads followed by async overwrites. The absence of the newly exposed scale overlap, fewer active token tiles, and different generated code can explain a much lower manifestation rate. Clean stress runs do not establish a stronger ordering guarantee.

For **question 4**, these are the remaining edges I would audit from the excerpts:

- **`ws->x2` / `ws->x2s` publication → bulk reads.** The producer-side global proxy fences are in the appropriate place after acquisition. The important conditional is `pre_ready`: skipping the explicit acquire is valid only if the scheduler’s acquire is carried through a release publication of `sq`, a producer acquire, and the producer warp synchronization. A Boolean `ready` value alone is insufficient. Likewise, `last_ready` caching assumes the source remains unchanged for that entry and epoch.

- **Initial `ws->x13` / `ws->x13b` publication.** Their branches show no local acquisition. That can be correct with an established preceding-kernel dependency. Verify the preparation→decode dependency, especially under PDL; do not assume early launch implies available inputs.

- **TMA-written stage → consumer generic reads.** Successful completion observation on `full[s]` supplies the implicit async→generic bridge. Another explicit consumer-start fence is not indicated by this excerpt. [PTX completion semantics](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#asynchronous-copying-async-proxy).

- **`desc`, `sd_row`, `sd_f` publication.** These are generic→generic accesses. They need the normal publication chain, not an async proxy fence. Confirm `mbar_expect_tx` includes a releasing arrival, or that a separate releasing arrival covers those writes. The TMA completion notification alone does not publish arbitrary earlier generic stores.

- **Barrier initialization.** Audit the omitted initialization/publication sequence separately. CUTLASS explicitly fences barrier initialization; a later stage-reuse fence is not a substitute for a correct initialization protocol. [CUTLASS initialization](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/main/include/cutlass/pipeline/sm90_pipeline.hpp#L302).

- **Actual TMA descriptors `tr.w`, `tr.s`, `p.sw`.** If these are updated descriptors in global memory, they need the applicable **tensormap-proxy** publication/acquisition. `fence.proxy.async.global` does not replace that. The kernel-parameter case has different handling. [CUDA tensor-map usage](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/04-special-topics/async-copies.html#using-tensor-maps).

- **Output atomics → finalize/reset.** These are generic-proxy operations. Check `pdl_wait()` and the launch dependencies, including reuse of `ws->y` by the next call. Proper grid-dependency synchronization waits for prerequisite completion and global-memory publication; a launch trigger alone does not. [CUDA PDL semantics](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/04-special-topics/programmatic-dependent-launch.html).

I have reviewed the excerpts and upstream ordering references, not your generated SASS. Before closing the investigation, I would prioritize the **+128-byte layout shift**, the **compiler-clobber-only control**, and a **physical-stage transition trace**. Those should discriminate the remaining explanations more effectively than additional undifferentiated stress runs.
